1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,454
It’s quite hard to think of a city that doesn’t have a big river

2
00:00:04,454 --> 00:00:05,180
running through it.

3
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,838
If you think about the major cities in the world, Shanghai, New York, Mumbai,

4
00:00:09,838 --> 00:00:13,070
London, they’re nearly all built on rivers.

5
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:18,530
When these cities were established hundreds or even thousands of years ago, the rivers

6
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:21,120
were a big part of people’s lives.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:25,609
In 16th-century London, the quickest way to get from one part of the city

8
00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:27,360
to another was by river.

9
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:32,234
But people also used the river for fishing, as the water then was relatively

10
00:00:32,234 --> 00:00:32,615
clean,

11
00:00:32,615 --> 00:00:36,305
and they would also go on boat trips up and down the river just

12
00:00:36,305 --> 00:00:40,477
for pleasure, as a relaxing escape from the noise and bustle of the city

13
00:00:40,477 --> 00:00:41,540
streets.

14
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:47,430
But as industries developed and populations increased, city rivers suffered.

15
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:51,253
The rising number of people meant there was a huge increase in the amount

16
00:00:51,253 --> 00:00:53,990
of sewage discharged into the rivers.

17
00:00:53,990 --> 00:00:56,181
Rivers had always been used for this purpose,

18
00:00:56,181 --> 00:01:01,690
but when the number of inhabitants was so small that wasn’t such a

19
00:01:01,690 --> 00:01:01,740
problem.

20
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:06,832
However, as cities grew to over a million inhabitants, the impact on the rivers

21
00:01:06,832 --> 00:01:08,740
became more serious.

22
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:14,377
In addition, other types of pollution increased as factories were built beside the river

23
00:01:14,377 --> 00:01:18,410
and discharged their waste materials into the water.

24
00:01:18,410 --> 00:01:20,550
This got worse over time.

25
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:27,826
As recently as 1957, scientists at London’s Natural History Museum declared that the River

26
00:01:27,826 --> 00:01:33,017
Thames was ‘dead’ in biological terms, as the water was too filthy to support

27
00:01:33,017 --> 00:01:34,620
any kind of life.

28
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:42,109
But in recent years, as rivers lost their industrial function, cities have begun to recognise their true value

29
00:01:42,109 --> 00:01:44,780
and to take steps to clean them up.

30
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:48,889
For example, the River Thames is now cleaner than it’s been for a hundred

31
00:01:48,889 --> 00:01:50,440
and fifty years.

32
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:53,451
These days you can see seals swimming in the water

33
00:01:53,451 --> 00:01:58,308
and recently people had to try to rescue a whale which had got lost

34
00:01:58,308 --> 00:02:01,640
and swum up the river from the sea by mistake.

35
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:04,133
Unfortunately they didn’t succeed,

36
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:09,080
but the problem was disorientation rather than the quality of the water.

37
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:14,474
Then, all around the world, riverside areas are now seen as prime sites for

38
00:02:14,474 --> 00:02:15,520
development.

39
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:21,477
Warehouses that were once used for storing goods are now being converted into expensive restaurants

40
00:02:21,477 --> 00:02:24,727
and also into apartments with river views,

41
00:02:24,727 --> 00:02:29,380
which are in great demand and sell for astronomical prices.

42
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:34,090
In Los Angeles, on the west coast of the USA, an architect has plans

43
00:02:34,090 --> 00:02:38,498
to revitalise the banks of the river and to make a park there which

44
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:44,250
can provide facilities for sports as well as a natural environment for relaxing in.

45
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:49,283
It’s also hoped that the riverside can be used for other purposes – it’s

46
00:02:49,283 --> 00:02:55,459
been proposed that facilities could be provided for displaying projects related to various kinds

47
00:02:55,459 --> 00:02:59,670
of art that have been produced by local people, for example.

48
00:02:59,670 --> 00:03:03,177
In the city of Paris, during the summer months of July

49
00:03:03,177 --> 00:03:07,706
and August, all the traffic is banned from the roads by the sides of the river

50
00:03:07,706 --> 00:03:13,518
and the banks are transformed into beaches, where people can relax in deckchairs under

51
00:03:13,518 --> 00:03:15,842
potted palm trees, sunbathe,

52
00:03:15,842 --> 00:03:19,810
or buy a drink or a snack while enjoying the view.

53
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:23,902
But to make the most of our rivers in our increasingly crowded cities, we

54
00:03:23,902 --> 00:03:28,196
need to allow them to regain their original purpose and be used as a

55
00:03:28,196 --> 00:03:32,930
means of transport, reclaiming our streets from cars and lorries.

56
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:37,924
To do this we’ll have to shift more traffic back to the river,

57
00:03:37,924 --> 00:03:43,760
but this time cleanly and silently, making the most of modern technology.

58
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:49,417
Already more than two billion passengers use the ferry to travel in cities around

59
00:03:49,417 --> 00:03:53,730
the world like Istanbul, San Francisco and New York.

60
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:56,660
And these numbers are set to rise further.

61
00:03:56,660 --> 00:03:59,699
Admittedly, it’s not a fast way of travelling,

62
00:03:59,699 --> 00:04:03,120
but neither is a car when it’s stuck in traffic.

63
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:08,394
Of course, passenger traffic on roads might decrease as more people start working from

64
00:04:08,394 --> 00:04:09,260
home.

65
00:04:09,260 --> 00:04:14,555
But another recent development, the huge rise in online shopping, has meant that another

66
00:04:14,555 --> 00:04:17,283
form of urban traffic just keeps on growing,

67
00:04:17,283 --> 00:04:19,870
and that’s deliveries.

68
00:04:19,870 --> 00:04:25,360
Trucks and vans in the city pollute and double park while dropping off parcels.

69
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:31,000
Imagine using the immense capacity of shipping to take these trucks off the road.

70
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,660
One freight barge can replace 44 large trucks, uses far less energy

71
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:39,390
and causes less pollution.

72
00:04:39,390 --> 00:04:43,746
When the barge docks at the riverside, the parcels could be taken the last

73
00:04:43,746 --> 00:04:50,070
few kilometres to their final destination on cargo bikes – electric ones, of course.

74
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:53,270
This is already happening in the Dutch city of Amsterdam.

75
00:04:53,270 --> 00:04:58,611
And in future, the final stage could even be carried out by drone, although

76
00:04:58,611 --> 00:05:00,760
at present this isn’t allowed.

77
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:05,000
Wouldn’t it be great to unblock our city centres in this way!

78
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,490
Looking further ahead …
